  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual diagram showing how stem surface moisture influences methane (CH₄) exchange between a tree trunk and the atmosphere. The trunk is split into two contrasting conditions: a moist surface with high interception on the left and a dry surface with low interception on the right. A dashed vertical line separates these conditions.\n\nRed arrows indicate methane movement across the bark, while blue curved arrows suggest internal circulation or mixing within the stem, and blue arrows from the roots show upward transport from belowground sources. The main visible comparison is that the size and direction of the red arrows change with stem moisture and along stem height. Near the lower trunk, larger outward arrows suggest stronger methane release, especially compared with the upper stem where arrows are smaller and may indicate weaker exchange. The moist and dry sides appear to differ in how much methane is emitted or retained, implying that intercepted water on the bark modifies gas transfer across the stem surface.\n\nOverall, the image communicates that methane emitted by trees is not fixed, but depends on both internal transport from roots and the moisture state of the stem surface.",
